<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Hilario López</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Hilario López was a Colombian general and Liberal politician who served as president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-new-granada">Republic of New Granada</a> from 1849 to 1853. His government carried out major liberal reforms, abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> from 1 January 1852, expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and separating more of public life from the church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-hilario-lopez.jpg" alt="José Hilario López" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Jos%C3%A9_Hilario_L%C3%B3pez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Hilario López Valdés was born on 18 February 1798, the son of José Casimiro López and Rafaela Valdés y Fernández, and studied at the seminary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> under the scholar José Félix de Restrepo. At fourteen he left school to join the revolutionary army as a cadet and fought at Alto Palacé, Calibío, Tacines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasto">Pasto</a>. Taken prisoner by the Spanish army at La Cuchilla del Tambo, he was court-martialled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> for treason and sentenced to death, but the sentence was commuted to service in the royal army. He was pardoned and freed on 24 July 1819 after his aunt Eusebia Caicedo intervened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1820 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> made him a lieutenant of the new Boyacá Battalion, and he rose to captain during the Northern Campaign in what is now Venezuela. Francisco de Paula Santander then appointed him military chief of Cauca province, and he became a lieutenant colonel on 6 April 1823. After the Septembrine Conspiracy of 1828 he rebelled against Bolívar and joined José María Obando, until the Pact of Juanambú of 2 March 1829 brought a pardon. In 1830 the two rose again against the military government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-urdaneta">Rafael Urdaneta</a>, a revolt ended by the Apulo Agreement of 28 April 1831. He later served as military chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, governor of Cartagena, secretary of war and navy, envoy to the Papal States, foreign secretary and senator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Elected on 7 March 1849 with the backing of the artisans and their democratic societies, López also passed an agrarian law and supported freedom of the press and a federal state. Resistance to abolition led to a conservative rising in Cauca under Julio Arboleda, which government forces crushed; in Cali the defeat of the landowners was followed by attacks on farms by former slaves and peasants. His government also dissolved the Amerindian resguardos, against the wishes of the democratic societies.</p><p>In 1854 he joined the armies that deposed General José Maria Melo. In the civil war of 1859 he sided with the radical liberals, and in July 1863 he became president of Tolima at Neiva. Defeated by Tomas Cipriano de Mosquera in 1865, he was made army chief after Mosquera's fall in 1867, then retired to his farms, where he died on 27 November 1869.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Hilario López” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The liberal president <b>José Hilario López</b> carried out major reforms, abolishing slavery in 1851 and expelling the Jesuits.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-new-granada">Republic of New Granada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. Died in Neiva, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=2.45,-76.61&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Popayán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Hilario López"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 4790h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 4790h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=2.45,-76.61&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
